@vthumbe1503 Aliasing would drop the copy without touching the common amax kernel, so I tried it before changing the PR. It breaks NVFP4 under torch.compile.

The prototype is this PR's base (`fac8d4a9`) with only `quantizer.cpp` changed: `create_tensor` and `convert_and_update_tensor` set `amax_columnwise = amax_rowwise` when there is a single amax (no RHT, no row scaling), and the existing pointer check in `quantize_impl` then skips the copy (diff below). It is built the same way as `main` (`NVTE_FRAMEWORK=pytorch NVTE_CUDA_ARCHS=120a`, CUDA 13.4, PyTorch 2.16.0.dev20261016+cu134) and run on the same RTX 5050 (SM 12.0):

| | `main` | aliasing |
|---|---|---|
| `test_torch_compile.py -k nvfp4` | 47 passed | **19 failed**, 28 passed |
| `tests/pytorch/nvfp4` | 8,576 passed, 754 failed¹ | same tests, same outcomes |
| `test_numerics.py`, `test_sanity.py`, `test_quantized_tensor.py`, `test_fusible_ops.py` with `-k nvfp4`; `test_weight_swizzle_in_layers.py`; `test_nvfp4_dual_amax.py` from this PR | 137, 3,344, 63, 257, 36, 24 passed | same |

¹ The SM120 failures listed in the PR description; they fail on both builds.

All 19 failures are the aliasing check of `torch.library.custom_op` (`_c_check_aliasing_constraint`): 16 in `linear_base` (`test_te_linear_compiles`, `test_te_linear_compiles_non_2d_input`, `test_te_linear_compile_is_first_microbatch`, with and without RHT) and 3 in `bias_backward_base` (`test_te_ops_linear_bias_compile`, `test_te_ops_bias_nvfp4_layout[2d]`, `test_te_ops_linear_bias_cold_cudagraphs`):

```
RuntimeError: linear_base (...): The output of this custom operator (1) must not also be an input to this custom operator and (2) may not alias any inputs to this custom operator or other returns.
```

`_flatten_value` in `dynamo/custom_op.py` returns each inner tensor from `__tensor_flatten__` as its own entry of the op's `Tensor[]` return, so the aliased `_amax_rowwise` and `_amax_columnwise` become two returns that share storage.

A second case: `convert_and_update_tensor` keeps both amax buffers when their sizes match (one element each), so a tensor allocated with aliased amaxes and then quantized in place by an RHT quantizer gets both amaxes in one float. Compared with a fresh RHT quantization of the same input by the same quantizer:

| input (256x1024 BF16) | row-wise / column-wise amax, expected | aliasing |
|---|---|---|
| `randn` | 4.375 / 5.03125 | 5.03125 / 5.03125, row-wise data and scales differ |
| `randn` with one element set to 64 | 64 / 16.875 | 64 / 64, column-wise data and scales differ |

The amax reduction itself is fine with aliasing: with `with_amax_reduction=True` on two ranks (RTX 5080 Laptop GPU and RTX 5050, NCCL 2.32.3), passing the aliased buffer twice to `allreduce_coalesced` gave the global maximum in all 500 iterations on both ranks.

So aliasing needs more than the `quantizer.cpp` change: the compile flatten/unflatten path, and the fake layout from `inner_tensor_specs`, would have to know that `_amax_columnwise` is `_amax_rowwise` (or the op would clone it at the boundary, which brings the copy back under torch.compile), and `convert_and_update_tensor` would have to split the pair for an RHT quantizer. Given that, I'd keep this PR's approach, where the reduction writes both buffers and nothing aliases. If you still prefer aliasing, I can implement it with those changes. Which would you like?

